(* Groups pages **************************************************************)
^page{Groups}{^T{G1MRA groups page}}{^groupsPrimary}{^groupsSecondary}{
^cbox{^tbox{^h1{^T{G1MRA groups}}
^PAR{^T{G1MRA has many local groups which range in size from a few friends who run together, up to large scale groups which have their own separate subscription and committee structure, run exhibitions and manage portable tracks.

Groups arrange their own meetings, many of which are in private gardens. This is a public website, so we do not publish names or addresses for these events. The best way to find out about activities in your area is to locate your nearest group, and then email the group contact using the address in the listing below. Alternatively, email enquiries to the G1MRA groups coordinator as ^g1mail{groups}.

We also have global special interest groups such as the Gauge One 3D Circle which are not limited to geographical regions. These kinds of groups operate mostly via email and electronic forums, and have members all over the world.

^em{If you are a group coordinator and wish to update your entry, please email ^g1mail{web}}.
}}
^vskip
^groups
}}}

(* Resources page ************************************************************)
^page{Resources}{^empty}{^resourcesPrimary}{^resourcesSecondary}{
^cbox{^tbox{^PAR{
^h1{^T{Resources}}
^T{Under this page you will find a library of useful information. 

Our} ^link{^T{safety}}{Safety^us^lang.html} ^T{advice includes a discussion of the risks arising from poor fuel management, boiler testing and diagnostic diagrams for use when an engine is misbehaving. 

Track and wheel standards are vital for reliable operation, especially in the garden where track alignments may be imperfect. Our} ^link{^T{standards}}{Standards^us^lang.html}^T{ page includes drawings illustrating our traditional and fine-scale standards.

Some folk collect G1 models that remain pristine in display cases, but most of us want to see our engines run. The} ^link{^T{tracks}}{Tracks^us^lang.html}
^T{page lists our portable tracks which appear at exhibitions and group running days. For exhibition managers wanting to book a G1 running track, we give the footprint of each portable track and a contact email address. 

We also list model engineering and modelling clubs which have permanant tracks: usually you would need to join the club concerned to get access.
}
^hrule

^T{You can read about the history of Gauge One railway modelling and of our association} ^link{^T{here}}{History^us^lang.html}. ^T{The}
^link{^T{Gauge 2}}{Gauge2^us^lang.html} ^T{pages document the mostly long-gone world of Gauge 2 model railways - our sister gauge. 

We have a list of useful other sites}
^link{^T{here}}{Connections^us^lang.html} ^T{grouped by category. The forums and video channels,in particular, are well worth joining.

The} ^link{^T{features}}{Features^us^lang.html} ^T{section includes descriptions and drawings of prototype rolling stock, advice on garden railway construction and some accounts of well regarded models.}

^hrule

^T{Our quarterly Newsletter and Journal is a substantial newstand-quality magazine, filled with technical articles, reports of meetings and supplier information. A complete article index of the G1MRA newsletter, covering all issues from 1947 to the present is} ^link{^T{here}}{images/index.xls}.

^T{G1MRA members can download backnumbers as encrypted Zip files from} ^link{^T{downloads}}{Downloads^us^lang.html} ^T{page. When you open the download you will be prompted for a password that you can find in your hard-copy of the most recent newsletter issue, or request the password by emailing} ^g1mail{web}.

^cbox{
^link{^img{TN285.png}{G1MRA NLJ 285}}{https://www.cs.rhul.ac.uk/~adrian/encrypted/G1MRA_285.pdf.zip}
